<h1 style=3D"font-size:26px;line-height:1.2;">Accessible colour systems</h1>
<p>Hi Anna,</p>
<p>This week, we=E2=80=99re looking at what makes a colour system useful beyo=
nd the palette: reliable contrast testing, clear naming, and guidance that ho=
lds up when a design moves into production.</p>
<h2 style=3D"font-size:20px;">Test the combinations, not just the swatches</h=
2>
<p>Contrast checks work best when they reflect actual use. Review text agains=
t its intended background, include interaction states, and check that colour =
isn=E2=80=99t the only way important information is communicated.</p>
<h2 style=3D"font-size:20px;">Name colours by their role</h2>
<p>Names such as =E2=80=9Ctext primary=E2=80=9D and =E2=80=9Csurface muted=E2=
=80=9D help teams understand intent. Keep descriptive palette names underneat=
h, so a change in hue doesn=E2=80=99t require a change in meaning.</p>
<h2 style=3D"font-size:20px;">Featured case study</h2>
<p>Our featured case study follows the South Harbour archive catalogue, exami=
ning how contrast testing and colour naming can support a coherent, accessibl=
e catalogue experience.</p>
<p>As you read, consider which colour decisions in your own system are explic=
